# Copywriting Standards — The Editor

Read `00-brand-core.mdc` first. This file enforces the voice at the sentence level.
use ';" instead of "—" in files that are .tsx except for comments.  the reader cant be seeing this "—" being used. 

## Banned words and phrases

If any of these appear in new or edited copy, rewrite the sentence. They are the fingerprint
of generic agency/SaaS writing and instantly break the brand's credibility:

- "unlock," "leverage," "empower," "seamless," "game-changer," "solutions," "elevate your"
- "at your fingertips," "take your business to the next level," "we're passionate about"
- "detailed breakdown," "expand each feature," "compare features," "learn more" as a CTA
- "boost your business" (ironic — "boosting" is the thing we mock elsewhere on the site)
- Any sentence that describes the brand's own honesty or attitude out loud
  (e.g. "we'd rather tell you the truth than..." — just tell them, don't narrate it)

## Sentence rhythm rules

- Short sentences do the damage. Long sentences explain. Default to short.
- Second person, always. The visitor is "you," addressed directly, not "our clients" or "business
  owners."
- One idea per line where possible. Let white space do some of the punching.
- Punchlines land at the end of a section, not buried mid-paragraph.

## The escalation rule

Tone intensity is not flat across the page — it climbs. Early sections (hero, identity match)
can be dry and observational. Mid-page sections (agitation, mechanism) get confrontational.
Late-page sections (pricing, final CTA) should carry the hardest lines on the page. If a
section near the bottom reads softer than one near the top, that's a bug — flag it.

## Before / after (use this as the litmus test for new copy)

BAD (generic, explains itself):
> "We believe in transparency and always try to be upfront with our clients about what we can
> realistically deliver."

GOOD (Hard Port voice):
> "We don't have a hundred reviews. We're new. Take it or leave it."

## FAQ-specific rule

Every FAQ answer must match the aggression level of its question. If the question is blunt
("Why are you so rude?"), the answer cannot be a polite customer-service paragraph. Write the
answer as a comeback, not an explanation.

## Pricing / feature-comparison rule

Never present pricing tiers with neutral SaaS labels ("Compare Features," "Detailed
Breakdown"). Tier copy should keep doing the talking the tier names already do ("scared of the
water," "done playing small") — treat the comparison table as a section that needs writing,
not a UI component that needs filling in.
